{{- if lookPath "herdr" }}{{ output "herdr" "--skill" | trimSuffix "\n" }}

## Local rules (dotfiles, not generated by herdr)

- When to use herdr is decided by `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` (sections `# Agents` and `# SSH`); it
  overrides the "Use only when the user explicitly mentions Herdr" line in the description.
- Claude sessions: message them with `SendMessage`, never `herdr agent prompt`, which types
  into their input line.
- `herdr agent start ... -- "<prompt>"` rejects prompts with newlines
  (`invalid_agent_argument`): put the task in a file and pass a one-line prompt that names it.
  If the agent starts working right away, `agent start` can return `timeout` without keeping
  the name: check with `herdr pane read`, then `herdr agent rename <pane> <name>`.
- The user's terminal or NAS output: read it yourself with
  `herdr pane read <pane> --source recent-unwrapped --lines N` instead of asking them to paste it.
  Ask once per session which pane is which, then keep reading. Read only: never `send-text`,
  `send-keys` or `pane run` into their panes.
- Feature or design wishes for herdr: first use what the installed plugins offer (options,
  hooks such as radar's `render_hook`); new plugins only as second choice or on request.
- This skill is rendered by chezmoi and does not update itself. If a command from it fails or
  `herdr --version` is newer, run `chezmoi diff ~/.claude/skills/herdr/SKILL.md`, then (after
  asking the user) `chezmoi apply ~/.claude/skills/herdr/SKILL.md`. Never `herdr --skill >` into
  the target; local additions go only into this Local rules section.
